wiki_plot.text:
William Greaves presents footage of a 1972 party he convened at Duke Ellington’s townhouse in Harlem with living luminaries of the Harlem Renaissance, an event he regarded as the most important he captured on film. Greaves invited every surviving participant he could locate. Many had not seen one another for decades. Attendees included the painter Aaron Douglas; the artist and writer Richard Bruce Nugent; the poet Arna Bontemps; the musicians Eubie Blake and Noble Sissle; the photographer James Van Der Zee; and Ida Mae Cullen, the widow of Countee Cullen. They also included society editor Gerri Major, poet Frank Horne, artists Romare Bearden and Ernest Crichlow, actor Leigh Whipper, librarians Jean Blackwell Hutson and Regina Anderson, theatrical producer Irvin C. Miller, historians Nathan Huggins and John Henrik Clarke, activist Richard B. Moore, journalist Ted Poston, and writer George Schuyler.

They discuss other key figures who had died including Marcus Garvey, Langston Hughes, Zora Neale Hurston, Claude McKay, Paul Robeson, and Augusta Savage.
